  <page>
    <exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        Calculate the following limits.
    </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
       <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow -3^+}\frac{x+4}{x+3}</m>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>
            Note that we cannot plug in <m>x=-3</m> into <m>\frac{x+4}{x+3}</m>, because then we would be dividing by <m>0</m>.
            As <m>x</m> gets close to <m>-3</m>, <m>x+4</m> approaches <m>1</m>.
            As <m>x</m> gets close to <m>-3</m>, <m>x+3</m> approaches <m>0</m>.
            Hence
            <md>\lim_{x\rightarrow -3^+} \left|\frac{x+4}{x+3}\right| =\infty. </md>
            Note that, as <m>x</m> approaches <m>-3</m> <em>from above</em>, <m>x+3\gt 0</m>, and <m>x+4\gt 0</m>. Hence
            <md>\lim_{x\rightarrow -3^+} \frac{x+4}{x+3}=\infty. </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
       <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{3x^2+x-7}{x^2 + x -2}</m>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>
            We will divide top and bottom by the highest power of <m>x</m> in the denominator. 
            Here, that is <m>x^2</m>.
            <md>
                <mrow>\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{3x^2+x-7}{x^2 + x -2} \amp =
                \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{(3x^2+x-7)\frac{1}{x^2}}{(x^2 + x -2)\frac{1}{x^2}}</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \frac{3 + \frac{1}{x}-\frac{7}{x^2}}{1 + \frac{1}{x}-\frac{2}{x^2}}</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \frac{\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}3 +\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{x} - \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{7}{x^2}}{\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}1 + \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{1}{x}-\lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\frac{2}{x^2}} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \frac{3+ 0 + 0}{1+0+0}=3. </mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that a ball is dropped from the upper observation deck of the CN Tower, <m>450</m>m above the ground.
                The equation of motion of the ball is given by 
                <md>
                    s = f(t) = 4.9 t^2. 
                </md>
                Find the velocity of the ball after <m>5</m> seconds. (Use limits to calculate your answer).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <solution>
        <p>
            The velocity at the time <m>t = 5</m> is given by
            <md>
                v(5) = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{s(5+h)-s(5)}{h}.
            </md>
            Note that
            <m>
                s(5) = (4.9)(25)
            </m>
            and <m>s(5+h)=4.9(25+10h+h^2)</m>.
            Thus
            <md>
                <mrow>v(5) \amp= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{4.9(25+10h+h^2)-4.9(25)}{h} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp =  \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{4.9h(10+h)}{h}</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} 4.9(10+h) = 49\ \text{ft/s}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Note that, in this problem your solution should include units: feet per second;ft/s.
        </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
        <p>
            This is an alternate approach to the same question. Instead of directly calculating <m>v(5)</m> we can find the general formula for <m>v(t)</m>, and then evaluate at <m>t=5</m>.
            <md>
                <mrow>v(t) \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{s(t+h)-s(t)}{h} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{4.9(t^2 +2th + h^2)-4.9t^2}{h} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{4.9h(2t + h)}{h} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}4.9(2t + h) = 9.8t \text{ ft/s}. </mrow>
            </md>
            Hence, the velocity at <m>t=5</m> seconds is
            <md>
                v(5) = (9.8)(5)=49\text{ ft/s}.
            </md> 
        </p>
    </solution>
  </page>

  <page>


    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = x^2 - x.</m>
                Use the limit definition of the derivative to find a formula for <m>f'(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The formula for the derivative of <m>f</m> is
                <md>
                    f'(x) = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.
                </md>
                Hence
                <md>
                    <mrow>f'(x) \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{(x+h)^2-(x+h)-[x^2-x]}{h} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{x^2 + 2xh +h^2 -x-h-x^2+x}{h} 
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{2xh+h^2-h}{h} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}(2x + h - 1) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 2x -1. </mrow>
                </md>
                Hence <m>f'(x) = 2x - 1.</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The graph of a function <m>f(x)</m>, with <m>x</m> in the interval <m>[-4,4]</m> is given below.
                Use the graph to answer the following questions about the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                <image label="quiz2" width="35%">
                <sageplot>
                f(x) = 1/6*x^3 -2*x
                plt = plot(f(x), (x,-4,4),gridlines="major")
                plt
                </sageplot>
                </image>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.05in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On what interval(s) is <m>f'(x)</m> positive?
                </p>
            </statement>
       <solution>
            <p>
                The derivative <m>f'(x)</m> gives the slop of the tangent line to the curve <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x</m>.
                Thus, <m>f'(x)</m> is positive when <m>f(x)</m> is increasing.
                From the picture <m>f(x)</m> is increasing up until <m>-2</m> and <m>f(x)</m> starts increasing again at <m>x =2.</m>
                Thus, <m>f'(x)</m> is positive on 
                <md>
                    (-4,-2) \cup (2,4).
                </md>
                If we assume that <m>f(x)</m> continues to decrease before <m>x-4</m>, and <m>f(x)</m> increases after <m>x = 4</m>, we could say that <m>f'(x)</m> is positive on 
                <md>
                    (-\infty, 2) \cup (2,\infty).
                </md>
                I accepted either answer as correct.
            </p>
        </solution>

        </task>

 
        <task workspace="0.05in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On what interval(s) is <m>f'(x)</m> negative?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    By the same reasoning as in part (a), <m>f'(x)</m> will be negative where the graph of <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing.
                    From the picture, <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing between <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x=2</m>.
                    Thus, <m>f'(x)</m> is negative on the interval.
                    <md>
                        (-2,2).
                    </md>
                </p>

                <figure>
                    <caption>Plot of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f'(x)</m>. </caption>
                    <image label="quiz2_2" width="75%">
                    <sageplot>
                    f(x) = 1/6*x^3 -2*x
                    g(x) = 1/2*x^2 - 2
                    plt1 = plot(f(x), (x,-4,4),gridlines="major", legend_label="$f(x)$")
                    plt2 = plot(g(x), (x,-4,4), legend_label="$f'(x)$", color="red")
                    plt= plt1 + plt2
                    plt.set_axes_range(-4,4,f(-4),f(4))
                    plt
                    </sageplot>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>